]
}
; THE LIGHT SWING, and it is the RIGHT BUMPER -- button 10, RB/R1.
;
; THE SWORD IS ON THE RIGHT HAND SIDE OF THE PAD NOW, both halves of it: the
; fast swing under the index finger on RB, the heavy under the same finger on RT
; (see `heavy` below). That is the Souls arrangement rather than OOT's, and it is
; the one this game's moveset has actually grown into -- a light chain, a heavy,
; a charged release, a guard on the opposite trigger and four dodges. OOT put the
; sword on a face button because OOT's sword had one move.
;
; The face buttons are verbs now and nothing else: A talks, B dodges, X puts your
; gear away, Y jumps. A thumb that never has to leave the stick to swing is the
; whole point -- the old X-swings layout meant every attack was a thumb leaving
; the camera.
;
; The dash (guard + this) and the air slam (this, off the floor) stay here: they
; are the same finger asking for the same swing in a different situation, which
; is what a modified press is for. hero_combat.gd's `_tick_light_button` owns it.
;
; The HOLD went with the heavy. This button no longer charges anything -- tap it
; as fast as you can and the chain is all you get.
attack={
"deadzone": 0.2,
"events": [Object(InputEventMouseButton,"resource_local_to_scene":false,"resource_name":"","device":-1,"window_id":0,"alt_pressed":false,"shift_pressed":false,"ctrl_pressed":false,"meta_pressed":false,"button_mask":0,"position":Vector2(0, 0),"global_position":Vector2(0, 0),"factor":1.0,"button_index":1,"canceled":false,"pressed":false,"double_click":false,"script":null)
, Object(InputEventJoypadButton,"resource_local_to_scene":false,"resource_name":"","device":-1,"button_index":10,"pressure":0.0,"pressed":false,"script":null)
]
}
; THE HEAVY, and it is the RIGHT TRIGGER -- JoyAxis 5 (TRIGGER_RIGHT), an
; InputEventJoypadMotion like the sticks, so the 0.2 deadzone is how far it
; travels before it counts.
;
; ONE FINGER, ONE PULL, AND WHAT IT DOES IS WHATEVER IS IN THE RIGHT HAND:
;
;   sword, free            -> the heavy swing. The overhead, slower and harder
;                             than the chain on RB above.
;   sword, HELD            -> the wind-up, and the release is the spin. Elden
;                             Ring's R2 exactly: tap it for the heavy, hold it
;                             for the charged one, and the charge costs the
;                             recovery you were spending anyway.
;   sword, locked on       -> the JUMP ATTACK, which is OOT's Z+A and used to be
;                             on A. It only exists under a lock, and out of the
;                             lock's range it falls through to the heavy rather
;                             than going silent -- see hero_combat.gd.
;   bow / hookshot drawn   -> that item's trigger. Held: down aims, up looses.
;
; THE ITEM REPLACES THE SWORD ON THIS BUTTON and that is the design, not a
; collision: drawing something is what "temporarily" means. The way back out is
; the D-pad -- the sword is a stop on the same weapon ring the bow is on, so
; ← or → hands it back -- plus the light swing's own escape hatch, so a sticky
; draw can never trap you.
;
; It was `use_item` until this pass, and it did ONLY the item half -- with the
; sword out and nothing locked it was deliberately silent. A trigger that does
; nothing four fifths of the time is a trigger the player stops pulling.
;
; THE TWO TRIGGERS ARE THE PAIR, which is the whole reason it is here. BOTW puts
; the shield on ZL and the drawn item on ZR, and it is right: the left index
; finger holds a stance and the right index finger fires what that stance is
; aimed at. Aiming a bow with the left trigger and loosing it with a thumb makes
; you let go of the stick that was aiming.
;
; Keyboard is F: left hand, without leaving WASD.
heavy={
"deadzone": 0.2,
"events": [Object(InputEventKey,"resource_local_to_scene":false,"resource_name":"","device":-1,"window_id":0,"alt_pressed":false,"shift_pressed":false,"ctrl_pressed":false,"meta_pressed":false,"pressed":false,"keycode":0,"physical_keycode":70,"key_label":0,"unicode":0,"location":0,"echo":false,"script":null)
, Object(InputEventJoypadMotion,"resource_local_to_scene":false,"resource_name":"","device":-1,"axis":5,"axis_value":1.0,"script":null)
]
}
; Block is the other half of the mouse: LMB swings, RMB raises the shield. It
; used to be K and Shift only, with RMB on z_target, and nobody found it -- the
; lock-on moved to the middle button, which is the thing you can afford to hide.
;
; ON THE PAD THIS IS OOT'S Z, and it is the whole of it. The LEFT TRIGGER --
; JoyAxis 4 (TRIGGER_LEFT), an InputEventJoypadMotion like the sticks, so the
; 0.2 deadzone is how far the trigger travels before it counts as pressed.
;
; One pull does four things, because in the source material they were never four
; things:
;   - raises the shield                       (hero_combat)
;   - acquires the best target                (targeting.gd `_read_guard`)
;   - pins the facing, so the stick strafes   (Hero._tick_camera_facing)
;   - and with NOTHING lockable, puts the camera back behind the hero.
; Let go and all four drop. An index finger on a shoulder is exactly what Z was.
;
; It spent one commit on LB (button 9) with the trigger reassigned to a bare
; recentre. That was a mistake and this is the undo: guarding, looking at the
; thing you are guarding against, and facing down an empty corridor are one
; intention, and splitting them across two fingers made the player choose.
;
; HELD, and polled with is_action_pressed -- the axis reports its travel through
; the same deadzone a button's 0/1 goes through, so nothing downstream in
; hero_combat cares which kind of event it is.
shield={
"deadzone": 0.2,
"events": [Object(InputEventMouseButton,"resource_local_to_scene":false,"resource_name":"","device":-1,"window_id":0,"alt_pressed":false,"shift_pressed":false,"ctrl_pressed":false,"meta_pressed":false,"button_mask":0,"position":Vector2(0, 0),"global_position":Vector2(0, 0),"factor":1.0,"button_index":2,"canceled":false,"pressed":false,"double_click":false,"script":null)
, Object(InputEventJoypadMotion,"resource_local_to_scene":false,"resource_name":"","device":-1,"axis":4,"axis_value":1.0,"script":null)
]
}
; THE DODGE IS B/CIRCLE -- button 1 -- and it has the button to itself.
;
; It spent one pass sharing A/Cross with `interact`, arbitrated by Prompt
; (`claims_press` / `claims_hold`): while something was in reach, the thing in
; reach owned the press and nothing rolled. That arbitration is DELETED, along
; with both of those functions, and this is the undo of it. The argument for
; sharing was that a thumb at rest wants either "get out of the way" or "talk to
; that", never both at once. True, and still not worth it: it put a rule on the
; most reaction-critical button in the game, it cost you the roll away from a
; chest, and the whole of it existed to keep B free for `ui_cancel` -- which B
; still is, because a menu is up or it is not (see the note on `ui_cancel`).
;
; So the pad reads plainly now, with one verb per face button: A talks, B
; dodges, X drinks what you have selected, Y jumps, and the sword lives on the
; two right shoulder buttons. Nothing on this pad needs a rule to say which of
; two things a press meant.
;
; THE JUMP ATTACK IS NOT HERE ANY MORE either. It was "guard up, stick still,
; A", which was OOT's Z+A read literally; it is `heavy` under a lock now, which
; is one finger asking for one swing. What that leaves behind is better: a
; neutral stick under a lock or a guard is the BACKSTEP, on this button, with no
; stick rule to learn and nothing to fall between.
;
; THE DODGE IS A TAP AND A HOLD, and both live on this one action. Tap it and
; the hero rolls, on the PRESS, with no latency added. Hold it past
; Hero.sprint_hold_time (0.18 s) and he runs, spending stamina until the bar or
; the button gives out. That is Elden Ring's arrangement, and the consequence --
; a held button rolls and THEN runs -- is Elden Ring's too. Nothing here is a
; second action to bind; Hero._tick_sprint reads the hold off the same `context`
; the roll reads its press off.
;
; The KEYBOARD half is Shift, and it is NOT shared with anything: Space is `jump`
; and E is `interact`. Shift is where the whole genre puts a dodge/sprint on a
; keyboard, it is under the pinky that is already resting on WASD, and it was the
; only unbound modifier left. KEY_SHIFT is 4194325 -- one past KEY_PAGEDOWN
; (4194324), which you can check against move_up's KEY_UP (4194320) three actions
; up this file. Do not derive it from memory any more than you would a JoyButton;
; _padnames.tscn prints the live map for both devices.
context={
"deadzone": 0.2,
"events": [Object(InputEventKey,"resource_local_to_scene":false,"resource_name":"","device":-1,"window_id":0,"alt_pressed":false,"shift_pressed":false,"ctrl_pressed":false,"meta_pressed":false,"pressed":false,"keycode":0,"physical_keycode":4194325,"key_label":0,"unicode":0,"location":0,"echo":false,"script":null)
, Object(InputEventJoypadButton,"resource_local_to_scene":false,"resource_name":"","device":-1,"button_index":1,"pressure":0.0,"pressed":false,"script":null)
]
}
; USE THE SELECTED ITEM -- drink the bottle. X/Square, button 2.
;
; It does ONE thing and it does it with no ladder, no stance and no arbitration:
; whatever D-pad ↓ has stepped the selection to, this button uses. Empty bottle,
; nothing happens; no bottles at all, nothing happens. That is the whole action.
;
; This is Elden Ring's X, and the shape is the reason the design converged on it.
; A flask is the one thing you reach for WHILE something is trying to kill you,
; so it wants a thumb button that is never ambiguous -- not a shoulder you are
; already holding, and not a button that means something else when your hand is
; full. The sword lives on the two right shoulders; nothing on the right hand
; competes for this thumb.
;
; IT SPENT ONE AFTERNOON AS `sheathe`, a ladder of "put the nearest thing away":
; stow a drawn item, then sheathe the sword, then draw it again. That went with
; the quick slots. The sword is a stop on the weapon ring now (see the D-pad note
; below), so ← or → hands it back and the button that existed to un-draw things
; had nothing left to un-draw. The hip scabbard survives as a LOOK -- the hero
; wears the sword when his hand is full of something else -- and not as a verb.
;
; Keyboard is X, which is the letter on the pad button. There is no shortage of
; keys and matching the glyph is worth more than another home-row raid.
use_item={
"deadzone": 0.2,
"events": [Object(InputEventKey,"resource_local_to_scene":false,"resource_name":"","device":-1,"window_id":0,"alt_pressed":false,"shift_pressed":false,"ctrl_pressed":false,"meta_pressed":false,"pressed":false,"keycode":0,"physical_keycode":88,"key_label":0,"unicode":0,"location":0,"echo":false,"script":null)
, Object(InputEventJoypadButton,"resource_local_to_scene":false,"resource_name":"","device":-1,"button_index":2,"pressure":0.0,"pressed":false,"script":null)
]
}
; Jump. SPACE, because Space is the jump button in every game with a keyboard,
; and Y/TRIANGLE -- button 3, "Top Action" -- because it is the face button the
; right-trigger move left empty and because BOTW, which is the game this project
; is chasing, puts jump on the top-right face button too (its X).
;
; THIS ACTION SHIPPED WITH THE ABILITY, which is the rule §4 of the contract
; states and the reason there was no `jump` here for so long: an action bound to
; nothing is a button that lies. Hero._try_start_jump, Hero._apply_gravity's
; impulse channel, and the two `ex_jump*` clips all landed in the same commit as
; these four lines.
;
; It is a real jump and not OOT's contextual ledge-hop: 0.96 m of apex, 0.59 s
; in the air, air control the whole way (K.JUMP_SPEED). Buffered, so a press
; during a fall lands and jumps; coyote-timed off the same grace the fall
; animation already used, so walking off a ledge and pressing a frame late still
; jumps. Neither is polish -- they are the two things that make a jump button
; feel like it is reading the player instead of the physics.
jump={
"deadzone": 0.2,
"events": [Object(InputEventKey,"resource_local_to_scene":false,"resource_name":"","device":-1,"window_id":0,"alt_pressed":false,"shift_pressed":false,"ctrl_pressed":false,"meta_pressed":false,"pressed":false,"keycode":0,"physical_keycode":32,"key_label":0,"unicode":0,"location":0,"echo":false,"script":null)
, Object(InputEventJoypadButton,"resource_local_to_scene":false,"resource_name":"","device":-1,"button_index":3,"pressure":0.0,"pressed":false,"script":null)
]
}
; SELECT AND TALK, and A/Cross -- button 0 -- has it to itself now.
;
; This button has carried three other things at various points: the jump attack
; (gone to `heavy`, under the lock where OOT's Z+A lived), the dodge (gone to B,
; see `context`), and stowing a drawn item (gone to the D-pad weapon ring, which
; hands the sword back without a button that only means "undo"). What is left is
; the one thing a player at a chest, a vendor or an NPC is pressing it for.
;
; A shares this button with ui_accept, which has always been safe: trade_screen's
; accept edge starts DISARMED, so the press that opens a screen cannot also
; confirm inside it. That is the ONLY arbitration left on this button, and it is
; a one-frame one inside a single screen rather than a rule about the world.
;
; Keyboard E, which is its own key and shares nothing.
interact={
"deadzone": 0.2,
"events": [Object(InputEventKey,"resource_local_to_scene":false,"resource_name":"","device":-1,"window_id":0,"alt_pressed":false,"shift_pressed":false,"ctrl_pressed":false,"meta_pressed":false,"pressed":false,"keycode":0,"physical_keycode":69,"key_label":0,"unicode":0,"location":0,"echo":false,"script":null)
, Object(InputEventJoypadButton,"resource_local_to_scene":false,"resource_name":"","device":-1,"button_index":0,"pressure":0.0,"pressed":false,"script":null)
]
}
; THE D-PAD IS THE WHOLE EQUIPMENT SCREEN, AND THERE IS NOTHING TO BIND.
;
; This is Elden Ring's D-pad minus the row this game has no content for (its ↑
; is the spell switch; ours is the loadout, which ER puts on a Y hold). LB IS
; DELIBERATELY EMPTY and is where spells go if they are ever built -- casting is
; a left-hand verb in every game this one borrows from, and leaving the button
; unbound is cheaper than binding it to something that then has to move:
;
;   ← / →   step the RIGHT HAND backwards / forwards through the weapons you
;           own: sword, bow, hookshot. The sword is a stop on that ring like
;           anything else, which is the answer to "how do I get my sword back"
;           and the reason there is no sheathe button any more.
;   ↓       step the selected ITEM -- the bottles. Selecting is not using.
;   ↑       swap the loadout SET, primary <-> alternate.
;
; X uses the selected item. RB and RT use whatever is in the right hand. Nothing
; on the D-pad fires anything, and that split is the point: Ocarina's C-button
; both equipped AND fired, so pulling the bow out was also loosing an arrow at
; whatever you happened to be facing.
;
; THE QUICK-SLOT BINDING SYSTEM IS GONE and this is what replaced it. Four
; bindable buttons, an ownership gate, an auto-bind on pickup and a whole card on
; the equipment screen existed so the player could decide WHICH DIRECTION the bow
; was on. A cycle has no such question, and the thing it costs -- direct access,
; two presses instead of one when you own three weapons -- is a cost the source
; material only avoids by having eight C-buttons' worth of screen furniture.
;
; ← is prev and → is next because that is the direction the icons move on screen,
; and the keyboard row is 1 2 3 4 reading around the pad the same way.
